# `ragazzi emarginati e a rischio': the youths marginalised and at risk. A
# participle that is no adjective to the lesson cannot be joined to one by `e',
# so `emarginato' (an adjective of apertium-ita.ita.dix) is the adjective too
<e r="RL"><p><l>marginalised<s n="adj"/></l><r>emarginato<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
# `persone impegnate nel recupero dei giovani emarginati': committed people --
# `impegnato' is an adjective of apertium-ita.ita.dix with no English word, and
# the participle of `impegnare' is pledged, which Spanish says `prometidas'
<e r="RL"><p><l>committed<s n="adj"/></l><r>impegnato<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
# `ragazzi provenienti dai ghetti': the dictionary's meaning for `proveniente'
# is resulting, which Spanish says `resultantes'; the youths come from the
# ghettos. Both ways, so that it is the first meaning of the word
<e><p><l>originating<s n="adj"/></l><r>proveniente<s n="adj"/><s n="mf"/></r></p></e>
# the Abogacía report (AnCora CESS-CAST-P-19991001-61), from Spanish: the Italian
# word for the honorarium the Spanish entries read; `onorario' has its paradigm
# in apertium-ita.ita.dix and no English word
<e r="LR"><p><l>honorarium<s n="n"/></l><r>onorario<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
# `se les retiene el 20%': retains -- the dictionary's `ritenere' says it first
# and is a believing; `trattenere' has its paradigm in apertium-ita.ita.dix and
# no bilingual entry. Both ways, so that it is the first word for `retain'
<e><p><l>retain<s n="vblex"/></l><r>trattenere<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# `la imagen distorsionada' (the Abogacía report, from Spanish): the participle
# of `distort' is `distorto', never `falsato'
<e><p><l>distort<s n="vblex"/></l><r>distorcere<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# `Los abogados deben ser justamente retribuidos' (the Abogacía report, from Spanish):
# the Italian monolingual dictionary has `remunerare' and no bilingual line for it
<e><p><l>remunerate<s n="vblex"/></l><r>remunerare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# the Makula interview (VIT-9381..9414, Italian into Spanish): words that have a
# paradigm in apertium-ita.ita.dix and no English word.
# `con le pinne': the fins of a swimmer; Spanish has `fin' as `aleta'. Both ways
<e><p><l>fin<s n="n"/></l><r>pinna<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
# `più efficiente': the dictionary says `efficient' as `efficace' (and Spanish's
# `eficiente' is read as it); only read, so that the word written stays the dictionary's
<e r="RL"><p><l>efficient<s n="adj"/></l><r>efficiente<s n="adj"/><s n="mf"/></r></p></e>
# `dev'essere immediato, tempestivo': timely, which Spanish says `oportuno'
<e r="RL"><p><l>timely<s n="adj"/></l><r>tempestivo<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
# `la trachea resta chiusa': Spanish has `trachea' as `tráquea'. Both ways
<e><p><l>trachea<s n="n"/></l><r>trachea<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
# `il termine di quattro minuti rimane inderogabile': a term that cannot be waived
# is binding; the dictionary's `mandatory' is Spanish's `obligatorio'. Only read
<e r="RL"><p><l>mandatory<s n="adj"/></l><r>inderogabile<s n="adj"/><s n="mf"/></r></p></e>
# `una bibita ghiacciata': a drink, which Spanish says `bebida'; ice cold is
# `freezing' for the dictionary's `helado'. Only read
<e r="RL"><p><l>drink<s n="n"/></l><r>bibita<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>freezing<s n="adj"/></l><r>ghiacciato<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
